{"giant":{"id":"694037cc-f816-40a3-9685-7728b40e2179","word":"giant","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"giant","word":"gigante"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"giant","word":"gigantesco"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The San Juan market is Mexico City's most famous deli of exotic meats, where an adventurous shopper can hunt down hard-to-find critters …. But the priciest items in the market aren't the armadillo steaks or even the bluefin tuna. That would be the frozen chicatanas – giant winged ants – at around $500 a kilo.","ref":"2013 July 26, Nick Miroff, “Mexico gets a taste for eating insects …”, in The Guardian Weekly, volume 189, number 7, page 32:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[268,273]],"id":"f48711d9-ae2e-479f-854e-c19106243db7"}],"links":[["large","large"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["Very large."],"tags":["not-comparable"],"id":"1f791ca0-8bf9-4483-b3b2-82bc9ca5d7d6"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"word":"colossal"},{"word":"enormous"},{"word":"gigantic"},{"word":"immense"},{"word":"prodigious"},{"word":"vast"}]},"giaour":{"id":"5de9b0d0-20e1-4471-9e9b-fe2d293ffd02","word":"giaour","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"infidel","tags":["masculine"],"word":"giaour"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"And far beyond the Moslem's power / Had wrong'd him with the faithless Giaour.","ref":"1813, Lord Byron, The Giaour, a Fragment of a Turkish Tale, London: John Murray, page 10:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[71,77]],"id":"615e9b1e-bf81-4e20-a926-9ebc3e5ae74a"},{"text":"We men are not a race of freebooters or giaours; not when our argosies are prey and food to the evil fish-of-metal whose lair is a German U-boat.","ref":"1963, Thomas Pynchon, V.:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[40,47]],"id":"e0b25007-e365-4bf8-97ea-fa3ba0712a9a"},{"text":"I shudder in delight when I think of two-hundred-year-old books, dating back to the time of Tamerlane, volumes for which acquisitive giaours gleefully relinquish gold pieces and which they carry all the way back to their own countries[…].","ref":"2001, Orhan Pamuk, translated by Erdağ M. Göknar, My Name Is Red:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[133,140]],"id":"997ffea4-6dc3-4648-8a5e-145a3c06b336"},{"text":"Byron’s tale called The Giaour is supposed to be told by a Turkish fisherman who had been employed all the day in the gulf of Ægi’na, and landed his boat at night-fall on the Piræus, now called the harbor of Port Leonê.[…]The tale is this: Leilah, the beautiful concubine of the Caliph Hasson^([sic]), falls in love with a giaour, flees from the seraglio, is overtaken by an emir, put to death, and cast into the sea. The giaour cleaves Hassan’s skull, flees for his life, and becomes a monk.","ref":"2004, Ebenezer Cobham Brewer, “The Giaour”, in Character Sketches Of Romance, Fiction And The Drama, volume 2, page 85:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[24,30],[323,329],[422,428]],"id":"e027a904-a7a7-4805-80fb-03d061a1fa97"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English doublets","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English religious slurs","English terms borrowed from French","English terms derived from Classical Persian","English terms derived from French","English terms derived from Ottoman Turkish","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 3 entries","Pages with entries","Quotation templates to be cleaned","Terms with Albanian translations","Terms with Arabic translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Azerbaijani translations","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Hindi translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Indonesian translations","Terms with 