ntesses used to play on.","ref":"1828, Thomas Keightley, The Fairy Mythology, volume I, London: William Harrison Ainsworth, page 218:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[110,120]],"id":"43c6c52f-7a4e-440f-9496-afab34eac946"},{"text":"The giantess picked him up and gave him such a squeeze that he felt he was going to choke.","ref":"1926, Enid Blyton, The Book of Brownies:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,12]],"id":"2e6937a6-2abb-4a9b-a81e-0abb4ac993b9"},{"text":"The giantesses lift arms like the trunks of sycamores, each finger tipped with an amaranthine talon.","ref":"1981, Gene Wolfe, chapter VIII, in The Claw of the Conciliator (The Book of the New Sun; 2), New York: Timescape, →ISBN, page 72:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,14]],"id":"1b1375a9-01f1-4312-8961-081d54e74dba"}],"topics":["fantasy","human-sciences","mysticism","mythology","philosophy","sciences"],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms derived from Middle English","English terms derived from Old French","English terms inherited from Middle English","English terms suffixed with -ess (female)","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Odia terms with non-redundant manual transliterations","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with French translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with Irish translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Latvian translations","Terms with Manx translations","Terms with Odia translations","Terms with Old Norse translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Romanian translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Swedish translations","en:Fantasy","en:Female people","en:Mythological creatures","en:Mythology","en:Stock characters"],"links":[["fantasy","fantasy"],["mythology","mythology"],["female","